  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 4♥. South has 9 top tricks (the ♠A, 5 heart tricks, 2 diamond tricks, and the ♣A). If South plays carefully, he can establish and cash his tenth trick in North's long diamond suit.\n\nIt would be a mistake for South to duck the opening spade lead. The defense would switch to clubs, setting up 2 tricks there and making South's position virtually hopeless.\n\nAfter winning the ♠A South should draw trump immediately. Next he should attack diamonds. South needs the defenders' diamonds to divide 3-2 to have a chance. Even with this break, South must be careful.\n\nIf South cashes North's ♦A and ♦K, and then surrenders a diamond, North's long diamonds will be good, but South will have no way to reach the dummy.\n\nThe solution is for South to duck either the first or second round of diamonds from both hands. After regaining the lead South will have a diamond in his hand with which he can reach the dummy.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠K against 4♥. South has 9 top tricks (the ♠A, 5 heart tricks, 2 diamond tricks, and the ♣A). If South plays carefully, he can establish and cash his tenth trick in North's long diamond suit.",
    "It would be a mistake for South to duck the opening spade lead. The defense would switch to clubs, setting up 2 tricks there and making South's position virtually hopeless.",
    "After winning the ♠A South should draw trump immediately. Next he should attack diamonds. South needs the defenders' diamonds to divide 3-2 to have a chance. Even with this break, South must be careful.",
    "If South cashes North's ♦A and ♦K, and then surrenders a diamond, North's long diamonds will be good, but South will have no way to reach the dummy.",
    "The solution is for South to duck either the first or second round of diamonds from both hands. After regaining the lead South will have a diamond in his hand with which he can reach the dummy."
